      <text class="jtbd-micro" x="96" y="60" text-anchor="middle">rel+category</text>
      <text class="jtbd-micro" x="162" y="60" text-anchor="middle">ask store</text>
      <text class="jtbd-micro" x="228" y="60" text-anchor="middle">analyze</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="jtbd-strip-caption">
      Firehose in; brief out. Triage and a queryable knowledge base sit in the
      middle so analysis time is not spent sorting.
    </p>

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Primary user:</strong> a defense-news analyst who must stay current
      across programs, contracts, geopolitics, and technology, and brief others on
      what matters. Defense is a deliberately chosen vertical, not a claim of domain
      expertise. <strong>Design partner:</strong> me, dogfooding, as the first user
      and the early validation loop.
    </p>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Job to be done</p>
      <p>When a stream of defense news comes in, help me quickly know <strong>what
        matters</strong> and <strong>where it fits</strong>, so I spend my time
        analyzing instead of sorting.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Success metrics</h2>
    <p>Two kinds. They answer different questions.</p>

    <h3>User-outcome metrics (does it help the analyst?)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Auto-triage rate:</strong> share of incoming items correctly categorized automatically, so the analyst never hand-sorts them.</li>
      <li><strong>Coverage (recall):</strong> share of genuinely relevant developments surfaced rather than missed.</li>
      <li><strong>Answer usefulness:</strong> for a query, the agent returns a correct, cited answer, fast.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="ex-caption">These outcome metrics are defined, not yet measured. Measuring them honestly needs the analyst in the loop over real use, and this is a solo build. What is measured today is classification quality, below.</p>

    <h3>AI-quality metrics (can it be measured, not just demoed?)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Classification quality:</strong> per-field precision, recall, F1. On real, human-labeled text the current v3 classifier scores category <span data-metric="category_accuracy">94.4%</span> (macro-F1 <span data-metric="category_macro_f1">0.930</span>), operational domain <span data-metric="domain_accuracy">98.1%</span> (<span data-metric="domain_macro_f1">0.982</span>), and region <span data-metric="region_accuracy">94.4%</span> (<span data-metric="region_macro_f1">0.975</span>). Corroborated at n=300 with confidence intervals half as wide as the 54-snippet set allows.</li>
      <li><strong>Cost per unit of quality:</strong> escalating to the premium model was built and measured, not assumed. It moved zero rows at about 1.97&times; the cost, so the shipped path stays single-model. That is the second of three independent measurements landing on the same verdict, alongside the retired retrieval layer and the <a href="faithfulness-judge.html">judge-reliability study</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Retrieval quality (RAG):</strong> recall@k, answer groundedness, citation correctness.</li>
      <li><strong>Regression gate:</strong> an eval pass-rate threshold wired into CI, so a change that drops below it does not merge.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Roadmap (program framing)</h2>
    <p>Sequenced Now / Next / Later, harvested from delivery.</p>
    <svg class="nnl-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 72" role="img"
         aria-label="Program sequence: Now is shipped work, Next is queued work, Later is deferred work including explicit non-goals.">
      <title>Now shipped, Next queued, Later deferred.</title>
      <rect class="nn-now" x="2" y="6" width="80" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="nn-title" x="42" y="24" text-anchor="middle">Now</text>
      <text class="nn-micro" x="42" y="38" text-anchor="middle">shipped</text>
      <rect class="nn-next" x="90" y="6" width="80" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="nn-title" x="130" y="24" text-anchor="middle">Next</text>
      <text class="nn-micro" x="130" y="38" text-anchor="middle">queued</text>
      <rect class="nn-later" x="178" y="6" width="80" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="nn-title" x="218" y="24" text-anchor="middle">Later</text>
      <text class="nn-micro" x="218" y="38" text-anchor="middle">deferred</text>
      <text class="nn-micro" x="130" y="64" text-anchor="middle">harvested from delivery</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="nnl-plate-caption">
      Solid is shipped. Hairline is queued. Dashed is deferred, including
      the things I will not build.
    </p>
    <div class="roadmap">
      <div>
        <h3>Now</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Async enrichment loop: create, classify, idempotent writeback</li>
          <li>Integration tests across both seams (pytest, fully offline)</li>
          <li>Agent tool-layer contract (SYS-003), accepted and implemented</li>
          <li>Evals-as-CI: the classifier&rsquo;s gold evals gate every PR</li>
          <li>Product one-pager and this program view</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Next</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Evals-as-CI for kb-agent: extend the gate to retrieval</li>
          <li>A weekly status cadence from real progress</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Later</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Containerize and deploy the services</li>
          <li>OpenTelemetry observability across services</li>
          <li>Other verticals: an explicit non-goal, articulated not built</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Risk register</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Risk</th><th>Severity</th><th>Mitigation</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Enrichment lost if the background task fails or the process restarts (no broker redelivery)</td><td>Low</td><td>Tags are derived and reclassification is idempotent (namespaced-tag replace), so enrichment re-runs safely; note creation never fails on classifier errors</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Classifier accuracy ceiling (residual label ambiguity, not the model)</td><td>Medium</td><td>Do not escalate the model &mdash; measured at +0 rows for ~1.97&times; cost. Refine the rubric instead: a confusion breakdown found the misses clustered on one boundary and two clauses lifted category 90.7% &rarr; 94.4%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Breadth creep (verticals without depth)</td><td>Medium</td><td>Deep on one vehicle; other verticals are an explicit non-goal</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Planning theater (docs drift from delivery)</td><td>Medium</td><td>Keep artifacts thin and living, tied to real delivery only</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Silent contract drift (separate repos)</td><td><strong>High &mdash; this one happened</strong></td><td>Frozen wire contracts, but the enforcement was never real: each repo's tests assert against its own copy of the shape, so when the classifier added a field the coordinated consumer update was skipped and both builds stayed green. Open. The fix is one shared artifact both sides check against</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Simulated program (no real cross-team coordination)</td><td>Low (honesty)</td><td>Stated plainly; the reasoning and artifacts are real, the org is not</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="capacity-gate">I pre-registered six axes for a capacity review, then measured that nothing wrote to them.</h2>
    <p>
      The same evidence gate this record applies to a model choice, applied to
      the build capacity behind the work. On a fixed date I wrote the review
      down: six evidence axes, a close date, and a rule that nothing changes
      until the close. Then I let the axes decide.
    </p>

    <svg class="gp-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 72" role="img"
         aria-label="How the capacity review was run: pre-register the evidence axes before any evidence exists, freeze every change until the close date, then rule on the record that accumulated. The gate returned a step down.">
      <title>Pre-register the axes, freeze until the close, rule on the record.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="gp-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>
      <rect class="gp-box" x="2" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="gp-title" x="40" y="24" text-anchor="middle">PRE-REGISTER</text>
      <text class="gp-micro" x="40" y="38" text-anchor="middle">axes first</text>
      <path class="gp-edge" d="M 78 26 L 90 26" marker-end="url(#gp-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <rect class="gp-box" x="92" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="gp-title" x="130" y="24" text-anchor="middle">FREEZE</text>
      <text class="gp-micro" x="130" y="38" text-anchor="middle">no changes</text>
      <path class="gp-edge" d="M 168 26 L 180 26" marker-end="url(#gp-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <rect class="gp-box" x="182" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="gp-title" x="220" y="24" text-anchor="middle">CLOSE</text>
      <text class="gp-micro" x="220" y="38" text-anchor="middle">on the record</text>
      <text class="gp-micro" x="130" y="64" text-anchor="middle">the gate said step down</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="gp-plate-caption">
      Two design choices did all the work: the axes were written before the
      evidence existed, and nothing changed until the close.
    </p>

    <p>
      Those two choices are the whole method. Because the axes were written
      first, nothing could pick its own success criterion after the fact.
      Because every change was frozen until the close, nothing could be cut on
      a bad week or kept on a good anecdote. The close came early, on a full
      month of evidence, for the only reason that justifies closing early: the
      last stretch added no new evidence on any axis.
    </p>

    <h3>The results, by axis</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>This section reports by evidence axis, never by lane and never by
      name.</strong> An axis is a question. A lane is a seat, and a seat maps to
      somebody&rsquo;s product &mdash; so reporting by lane would turn a
      one-operator measurement into a public claim about a third party. That is
      the difference between a measurement and an opinion with a table around
      it.
    </p>
    <ul class="ledger">
      <li><strong>One axis produced repeated, measured value.</strong> Independent review loops raised real findings; most were confirmed and fixed before merge, and the noise rate was nil. This is the axis that earned its keep.</li>
      <li><strong>The capacity axis produced the opposite of what I expected.</strong> No friction events at all &mdash; and, going the other way, dated directives in my own operating rules telling sessions to spend the headroom rather than conserve it. Capacity was never the binding constraint.</li>
      <li><strong>The remaining axes produced no dated unique result</strong> in a month that gave them the chance to.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The rulings, as principles</h3>
    <ul class="ledger">
      <li><strong>Capacity that was never consumed is unproven, not proven.</strong> So the top tier steps down, and the first month on the lower tier is itself the next measurement.</li>
      <li><strong>A parked upgrade was declined</strong>, because no recorded event showed that capacity ever blocking anything.</li>
      <li><strong>A seat is retained for demonstrated unique value.</strong> Attachment to a tier is not evidence.</li>
    </ul>
    {/* Owner call, still open: whether this section names figures rather than
        only the mechanism. It ships with the mechanism and no figures. If that
        is ever released it is three sentences and nothing else — one above
        this block, one clause in the first ruling, one in the closing
        paragraph. These markers are Astro comments, not HTML comments, so they
        do not reach the published page: a note about what a page deliberately
        withholds should not itself be published. */}
    <p class="ex-caption">
      The limit on this reading, at the point of the claim: one month, one
      operator, one body of work, and no controlled comparison. It is a routing
      decision for me. It is not a verdict on anybody&rsquo;s product.
    </p>

    <p class="tradeoff">
      <strong>The result:</strong> the six axes were pre-registered and nothing
      ever wrote to them. There was no friction log, no tally file, and no
      dead-rows list, so the evidence had to be reconstructed at the end out of
      dated records. The gate still returned a clear answer, because the answer
      was lopsided. <strong>A closer result would have been unrecoverable.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      The lesson: pre-registration without an instrument is half a measurement.
      I built a gauge for this same capacity, and it does not write this log
      today. That is the open item, and naming it is the only honest place to
      end.
    </p>
    <p class="diagram-more"><a class="card-link" href="telltale.html">telltale, the instrument for the same capacity <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a></p>

    <h2>What it demonstrates</h2>
    <p>
      The scale is personal. The program is simulated. The source documents
      live in the architecture repo:
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/architecture/blob/main/product/one-pager.md">the product one-pager</a>
      and <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/architecture/blob/main/program/README.md">the program view</a>.
    </p>
